(vector (map (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:actor/robotics? true), (:adr/status :accepted), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "proceed with the machine operation" "proceed with machine operation" "authorize the machine operation" "authorize machine operation" "finalize the machine-operation decision" "finalize the machine operation decision" "declare the plant safety cleared" "declare plant safety cleared" "declare the plant floor safety cleared" "override the plant safety officer's judgment" "override the safety officer's judgment" "override plant safety officer judgment")), (:adr/id 2799008114), (:actor/domain :manufacturing/mineral-products-plant-scheduling-logistics-coordination), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :no-operator :no-plant :no-actuation :unknown-op :scope-excluded-action)), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:actor/isco-08 "8114"), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-safety-concern)), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:adr/body "# ADR 2799008114: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8114 Cement, Stone and Other Mineral Products Machine Operators plant scheduling/logistics-coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the ISCO-08 occupation-classification track (sibling to\nthe `cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8114\n-- Cement, Stone and Other Mineral Products Machine Operators** only.\nThis batch (Wave3) begins the 81xx/82xx plant-and-machine-operator\nrange after Wave0/1/2 (100% complete) and the entire 75xx craft-trades\nrange (also complete).\n\nA fresh `gh api repos/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8114` 404 check\nconfirmed no pre-existing repository before any work began -- a\ngenuinely fresh scaffold.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8114\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"8114\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Cement, Stone and Other Mineral\nProducts Machine Operators\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch.\nPrior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with\n`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`. This ADR and its companion registry edit\npromote the `8114` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**Heavy-industrial-machine safety dimension.** Cement/Stone and Other\nMineral Products Machine Operators run heavy industrial forming/\npressing/kiln equipment -- real physical-safety stakes (heavy-machinery\ncrush/entanglement injury) plus dust/heat exposure. This actor is\ndeliberately scoped to **plant scheduling/logistics coordination ONLY\n-- it never operates plant machinery, and it never finalizes a\nmachine-operation-execution decision or a plant-safety-clearance\ndecision, and it never overrides a plant safety officer's judgment**.\nThose actions are a hard, permanent block in the governor, independent\nof confidence and independent of which op was proposed -- never\nauto-commit-eligible, never merely gated behind human escalation. The\nclosed op allowlist itself never includes any op that directly\nfinalizes a machine-operation-execution decision or a\nplant-safety-clearance decision, or overrides a plant safety officer's\njudgment; on top of that, an independent content-based scope-exclusion\ncheck on the proposal's free-text fields hard-blocks any attempt to\nsmuggle such a decision through an otherwise-allowlisted op. This\nmirrors the same class of guardrail this fleet has already applied to\nother domains with an irreversible physical/safety stake (e.g. the\nsibling ISCO-08 7211 Metal Moulders and Coremakers foundry-coordination\nactor, `cloud-itonami-isco-7211`, whose casting/pouring-execution and\nfoundry-safety-officer-override scope exclusions are the closest\nprecedent for the industrial-plant-safety-domain shape used here).\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7211`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7211)\n(Metal Moulders and Coremakers: `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor`\nsplit, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set -- CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\nCONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, LICENSE (AGPL-3.0-or-later), README.md,\nSECURITY.md, docs/business-model.md, docs/operator-guide.md), freshly\ncloned and read in full in this session (`deps.edn` coordinates for\n`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph` copied verbatim, same `:git/sha`\npin). `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8112` (the closer sibling\nsuggested as the primary reference, Mineral and Stone Processing Plant\nOperators) did not yet exist at the time of this session (fresh `gh api`\n404), so the fallback reference (`cloud-itonami-isco-7211`) was used.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8114` as an OSS plant\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for independent cement, stone\nand mineral products machine-operator crews, following the SAME\ngoverned-actor architecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor\nin this fleet: **Mineral Products Plant Coordination Advisor ⊣\nMineralPlantCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with\nan independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger. `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` is deliberately NOT a `deps.edn` dependency of this actor\nrepo -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry depends on\nit.\n\nThis actor is **plant scheduling/logistics coordination ONLY, never\nmachine-operation-execution or plant-safety-clearance authority** -- it\nmust NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising any decision that directly finalizes a\nmachine-operation-execution decision or a plant-safety-clearance\ndecision, or overrides a plant safety officer's judgment.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- production-run/inventory/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a machinery-hazard/heat-dust-\n  exposure/equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- raw-material/spare-parts procurement\n  proposal\n\n`mineralplant.governor/allowed-ops` is a closed four-op `#{}` set --\nany `:op` outside it is unconditionally rejected as `:unknown-op`\n(`mineralplant.governor-test/hard-on-op-outside-closed-allowlist`,\nexercised end-to-end through the actor graph), and the mock advisor\n(`mineralplant.advisor/mock-advisor`) can only ever emit whatever `:op`\nthe request itself carried (its `infer` function merges the request\nthrough unchanged, with no path to synthesize a foreign op).\n\n### Five HARD governor checks (`mineralplant.governor/hard-violations`, permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **No operator** -- the crew member (`operator-record`) must be\n   independently verified/registered before any action; an\n   unregistered operator is a HARD block (`:no-operator`).\n2. **No plant** -- the plant site (`plant-record`) must be\n   independently verified/registered before any action; an\n   unregistered plant is a HARD block (`:no-plant`).\n3. **No actuation** -- `:effect` must be `:propose`; any other value\n   is a HARD block (`:no-actuation`) -- the governor never dispatches\n   hardware itself and never operates plant machinery itself.\n4. **Unknown op** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op allowlist is\n   a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`).\n5. **Scope-excluded action** -- independent of the op-allowlist check,\n   any proposal whose `:op` names, or whose `:rationale` text contains,\n   a machine-operation-execution finalization action, a\n   plant-safety-clearance finalization action, or a\n   plant-safety-officer-judgment override action\n   (`mineralplant.governor/scope-excluded-ops` and\n   `mineralplant.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`: \"proceed with the\n   machine operation\", \"authorize the machine operation\", \"finalize\n   the machine-operation decision\", \"declare the plant safety\n   cleared\", \"override the plant safety officer's judgment\", and their\n   variants) is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:scope-excluded-action`),\n   evaluated unconditionally regardless of confidence. Full\n   finalization/execution ACTION phrases only, deliberately never bare\n   nouns such as \"cement\", \"kiln\" or \"dust\" -- see the self-trip guard\n   section below.\n\n### One always-escalate op, one cost-threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence. Heavy-machinery crush/entanglement, heat-exposure and\n  dust-exposure concerns are never auto-resolved.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `mineralplant.governor/supply-cost-threshold` (2000) always needs\n  human sign-off, independent of confidence (inclusive boundary\n  verified by `mineralplant.governor-test/ok-supply-order-at-threshold-\n  boundary` -- exactly-at-threshold auto-commits, above-threshold\n  escalates per `mineralplant.governor-test/always-escalates-supply-\n  order-above-threshold`). This is deliberately an ESCALATE, not a HARD\n  block -- a cost overrun is a business decision a human can approve,\n  unlike the machine-operation/safety-clearance scope exclusion which\n  is permanently blocked.\n- advisor confidence below `mineralplant.governor/confidence-floor`\n  (0.6) also escalates, for any op\n  (`mineralplant.governor-test/escalates-low-confidence`).\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"cement\", \"kiln\", \"dust\") can accidentally match inside the mock\nadvisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- exactly the risk this\nvertical's own vocabulary (every routine work-log/schedule/safety-flag\nproposal necessarily mentions words like \"cement\", \"kiln\" or \"dust\" in\nordinary production/hazard-reporting context) could trigger if phrased\ncarelessly. `mineralplant.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS, never bare nouns, and\n`mineralplant.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-\ntrip-on-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test asserting every\nop in the closed allowlist, driven through the default mock advisor\n(`mineralplant.advisor/mock-advisor`) with realistic field values\nincluding a `:hazard-type :dust-exposure-risk` field and a \"routine\ncement production-run task\" task string, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. Green on this session's test run -- the guard\nwas never observed to self-trip during verification.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8114\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to the\n  real repository (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-\n  isco-8114` / `cloud-itonami-isco-8114`), and `maturity-summary`'s\n  implemented count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the\n  registry via `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary` immediately before\n  the edit, not assumed, to account for concurrent sibling agents\n  landing changes to the same registry file in this batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`) run against the actual pushed,\n  freshly re-cloned repository: 21 tests, 45 assertions, 0 failures, 0\n  errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/raw-output` and in\n  the task report.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to this actor's own\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  depends on it.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-machine-cycle` or\n  `:finalize-safety-clearance` that is merely gated behind\n  `always-escalate-ops` rather than structurally excluded: rejected\n  outright per the task's explicit guidance and this fleet's\n  established precedent for irreversible-safety-stake decisions -- an\n  always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize a\n  machine-operation-execution or plant-safety-clearance decision exists\n  in the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. This actor\n  coordinates plant scheduling/logistics only; the capability to\n  finalize machine operation or plant safety clearance, or to override\n  a plant safety officer's judgment, must not exist at all, hence a\n  hard, permanent, content-based block rather than an escalation gate.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. [\"cement\" \"kiln\" \"dust\"]):\n  rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug class described\n  above -- this actor's own domain necessarily mentions \"cement\",\n  \"kiln\" and \"dust\" in ordinary work-log/schedule/safety-flag rationale\n  text, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking the actor's own\n  legitimate proposals. Phrased instead as full finalization/execution\n  action phrases, with a dedicated regression test proving the default\n  advisor's own proposals never self-trip.\n- A numeric confidence-only gate for `:log-work-record`/\n  `:schedule-crew-operation`/`:coordinate-supply-order` (no\n  operator-provenance/plant-provenance or machine-operation/\n  safety-clearance content checks): rejected as insufficient --\n  confidence measures the advisor's certainty about the proposal it\n  chose to make, not whether that proposal cites an unregistered\n  operator/plant or smuggles a machine-operation/safety-clearance\n  finalization decision in its free text, which is exactly why the\n  operator/plant-provenance and scope-exclusion checks exist as\n  independent, content-based, permanent HARD blocks regardless of\n  confidence.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- production-run/inventory/progress data\n   logging only. Requires an independently registered operator AND an\n   independently registered plant (HARD-blocked otherwise by checks\n   1/2). Not a machine-operation or plant-safety-clearance decision in\n   any respect -- it is a records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/shift/task *scheduling*\n   proposal (administrative logistics, e.g. \"assign operator to the\n   08:00-16:00 shift on the kiln line\"), never a machine-operation-\n   execution decision. Any free text that names a machine-operation-\n   execution finalization action, a plant-safety-clearance finalization\n   action, or a plant-safety-officer-judgment override is independently\n   HARD-blocked by check 5 regardless of what op was used to carry it.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a machinery-hazard/heat-dust-\n   exposure/equipment-condition concern to a human; ALWAYS escalates,\n   unconditionally, at every confidence level, NEVER auto-commit\n   eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which the robot may\n   surface a safety observation, and it explicitly never resolves,\n   decides, or acts on anything itself.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- raw-material/spare-parts\n   procurement proposal. Requires an independently registered operator\n   and plant (HARD-blocked otherwise by checks 1/2), and any proposal\n   above the registered cost threshold always escalates to a human,\n   independent of confidence. Not a machine-operation or\n   plant-safety-clearance action -- it is a purchasing-logistics\n   action.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, directly\nfinalizes a machine-operation-execution decision or a\nplant-safety-clearance decision, or overrides a plant safety officer's\njudgment -- confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`mineralplant.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) the content-based,\nphrase-driven scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op` field\n(`mineralplant.governor/hard-violations`,\n`mineralplant.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`mineralplant.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`), tested directly against\n`governor/check` (`mineralplant.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-\nfinalize-machine-operation-decision`,\n`mineralplant.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-declare-plant-\nsafety-cleared`,\n`mineralplant.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-plant-\nsafety-officer-judgment`, and the rationale-content variants), and (c)\nthe dedicated self-trip regression proving the default mock advisor's\nown proposals, across all four allowlisted ops, never accidentally trip\nthat same block\n(`mineralplant.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-\ntrip-on-scope-exclusion`)."), (:test/raw-output "Running tests in #{\"test\"}\n\nTesting mineralplant.actor-test\n\nTesting mineralplant.governor-test\n\nRan 21 tests containing 45 assertions.\n0 failures, 0 errors.\n"), (:adr/date "2026-07-18"), (:actor/cost-threshold-escalate-ops "{:coordinate-supply-order supply-cost-threshold}"), (:test/counts "{:total 21, :assertions 45, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/name "Cement, Stone and Mineral Products Plant Scheduling and Logistics Coordination Practice"), (:db/id -1), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :operator-provenance :plant-provenance :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation :machine-operation-safety-clearance-block)), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-8114"), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8114"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8114)), (:test/simulator :mineralplant.actor-test), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-8114 — Cement, Stone and Other Mineral Products Machine Operators Plant Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-8114)))
